  "live_records": {
    "q-95419": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95419",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(2{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
